{
  "summary": "This study investigates how dual twisted interfaces in CVD-grown trilayer MoS2 affect second harmonic generation (SHG). By fabricating nine distinct stacked configurations, researchers analyzed Raman and photoluminescence to identify stacking-dependent phonon and excitonic responses. Atomic-resolution techniques confirmed dual twisted interfaces and a supermoiré lattice. The study developed a framework linking dual interfaces to SHG intensity and polarization, establishing trilayer MoS2 as a tunable nonlinear optical platform.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Dual twisted interfaces in trilayer MoS2 create stacking-dependent phonon and excitonic responses",
    "Atomic-resolution techniques confirmed dual twisted interfaces and a supermoiré lattice structure",
    "The framework explains how dual interfaces govern SHG intensity and polarization across stacking configurations"
  ],
  "study_type": "CVD-grown twisted trilayer MoS2 configurations",
  "scientific_significance": "This work provides a foundational framework for engineering nonlinear optical responses in van der Waals materials through multi-interface twisted stacking."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigated how dynamic tensile stretching of auxetic scaffolds enhances mesenchymal stem cell-derived extracellular vesicles (EVs) for pulmonary fibrosis therapy. By mimicking lung breathing motions, the researchers engineered EVs enriched with miR-30 via YAP-dependent mechanotransduction. In vivo, these EVs reduced collagen deposition and improved lung structure in bleomycin-induced fibrosis models, demonstrating mechanical priming's role in optimizing EV therapeutic efficacy.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Dynamic stretching on auxetic scaffolds increased YAP nuclear accumulation in MSCs",
    "dEVs enriched with miR-30 inhibited myofibroblast activation and promoted M2 macrophage polarization",
    "In vivo administration of dEVs attenuated collagen deposition and restored lung architecture in bleomycin-induced fibrosis"
  ],
  "study_type": "In vitro and in vivo evaluation using a dynamic culture system with auxetic scaffolds",
  "scientific_significance": "This work establishes mechanical priming as a strategy to engineer EVs with specific therapeutic cargo for pulmonary fibrosis, highlighting the YAP/miR-30 axis and mitochondrial restoration as critical mechanisms for tissue repair."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigates why deep subcortical brain regions, such as the hypothalamus and brainstem, exhibit greater neuronal diversity than cortical areas despite having fewer neurons. Using whole-brain cell atlases, it proposes a dual subsystem model: a 'learning subsystem' (cortex, striatum, cerebellum) that scales through repetitive module replication, and a 'steering subsystem' (hypothalamus, pallidum, brainstem) that diversifies cell types for innate behaviors. The framework explains evolutionary divergence in brain architecture and potential implications for neurobiology and AI model design.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Deep subcortical structures have higher neuronal diversity than cortical regions with more neurons",
    "Learning subsystem scales via repetitive module replication for computational capacity",
    "Steering subsystem diversifies cell types to encode innate drives and reflexes"
  ],
  "study_type": "",
  "scientific_significance": "This framework provides a unified explanation for evolutionary brain architecture divergence between learning and steering subsystems, advancing understanding of neurodevelopmental mechanisms and potential AI model design principles."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigates layered spin-crossover metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) for light-induced control of two-dimensional quantum materials. The researchers integrated a Hofmann-type SCO MOF into van der Waals heterostructures with few-layer graphene, CrSBr, and NbSe2. Light-induced spin transitions modulated electronic transport in graphene, induced magnetic hysteresis in CrSBr spin-valves, and altered superconducting properties in NbSe2. These findings demonstrate strain-mediated control of quantum material functionalities via stimuli-responsive MOFs.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Light-induced spin transitions selectively switch graphene conductivity",
    "MOF integration triggers magnetic hysteresis in CrSBr bilayers",
    "Light modulates superconducting critical current and transition temperature in NbSe2"
  ],
  "study_type": "Electrical nanodevices with van der Waals heterostructures",
  "scientific_significance": "This work establishes layered spin-crossover MOFs as molecular switches for strain-mediated control of electronic, magnetic, and superconducting properties in two-dimensional quantum materials."
}

{
  "summary": "This study addresses age-related cortical bone loss by engineering a dual-targeted biomimetic periosteum. It mechanistically links inflammaging and mechanical decline to impair periosteal stem/progenitor cell function, then designs a membrane with immunomodulatory and mechanosensory modules to restore Piezo1 expression and activity. The biomaterial enhances cortical bone thickness and strength in aged and mechanically unloaded models, with effects dependent on Piezo1-mediated mechanotransduction.",
  "key_findings": [
    "2-N,6-O-sulfated chitosan repolarizes macro:ages and restores Piezo1 expression in periosteal stem/progenitor cells",
    "Electrospun fibers optimized for specific diameter activate Piezo1-dependent calcium influx and YAP signaling",
    "The biomaterial improves cortical bone thickness and strength in aged and hindlimb-unloaded models, requiring Piezo1 function"
  ],
  "study_type": "In vitro and in vivo models",
  "scientific_significance": "This work establishes a mechanoinflammatory framework for periosteal regeneration, demonstrating how integrated immune modulation and mechanosensory activation can restore load-bearing bone function during aging and mechanical disuse."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigates diploid human chromatin accessibility using Fiber-seq inferred regulatory elements (FIRE), revealing haplotype-selective chromatin accessibility (HSCA) and autosomal somatic epimutations. FIRE identifies HSCA in immune cells, particularly at the HLA locus, with disease-associated variants disrupting transcription factor binding. The findings demonstrate that somatic epimutations create mitotically stable chromatin alterations independent of genetic determinism.",
  "key_findings": [
    "FIRE provides a comprehensive quantitative map of accessible chromatin across the diploid human genome",
    "HSCA is prevalent at the HLA locus in immune cells, with disease-associated variants disrupting transcription factor binding",
    "Autosomal somatic chrom:epimutations propagate clonally to form mitotically stable chromatin alterations"
  ],
  "study_type": "Fiber-seq inferred regulatory elements (FIRE)",
  "scientific_significance": "The study establishes somatic epimutations as a mechanism for mitotically stable chromatin alterations that bypass genetic determinism, expanding understanding of epigenetic regulation in human development and disease."
}

{
  "summary": "This perspective addresses the reproducibility crisis in metal-bacteria interaction research despite its long-standing antibacterial applications. It identifies key sources of inconsistency, proposes a roadmap for mechanistic studies, and emphasizes causal perturbations, orthogonal measurements, and cross-laboratory validation to enhance reproducibility and advance bioengineering applications.",
  "key_findings": [
    "The reproducibility crisis in metal-bacteria interactions stems from inconsistent methodologies across studies",
    "A source-specific experimental roadmap is provided for mechanistic investigations",
    "Cross-laboratory validation and causal perturbations are critical for reliable results"
  ],
  "study_type": "",
  "scientific_significance": "This work establishes a framework for mechanistic, interdisciplinary research to resolve reproducibility issues in metal-bacteria interactions, advancing reliable bioengineering applications through standardized, causal approaches."
}

{
  "summary": "This study developed an implantable, degradable hydrogel evaporative cooler for repeated regional mild hypothermia in traumatic brain injury. The device achieved precise temperature regulation (33 ± 0.5 °C) for 4 hours daily over three days in rats. Results showed neuroprotection, enhanced neural repair markers, reduced lesion volume, preserved axonal integrity, and improved cognitive function at 28 days post-injury.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Repeated regional hypothermia via hydrogel cooler reduced traumatic brain injury lesion volume",
    "Treatment increased Nestin+/Ki67+ and Sox2+/Ki67+ progenitor-like cells",
    "Proteomic data indicated modulation of synaptic plasticity and neuroinflammation"
  ],
  "study_type": "In vivo rat model",
  "scientific_significance": "This approach demonstrates a feasible method for sustained, targeted hypothermia therapy in traumatic brain injury, offering potential for neuroprotection and functional recovery through repeated cooling cycles."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigated the mechanistic basis of sex-based differences in chronic liver disease (CLD) progression using metabolomics data from 9,254 individuals. It identified isoursodeoxycholic acid (isoUDCA) as a female-associated hepatoprotective bile acid derived from a bacterial consortium enriched by estrogen. The findings reveal isoUDCA promotes SREBP2 nuclear accumulation, upregulating STARD4 and CYP7B1 to enhance intracellular cholesterol handling, suggesting a microbiome-bile acid-liver axis that may explain sex disparities in CLD susceptibility.",
  "key_findings": [
    "IsoUDCA is a bile acid derived from a bacterial consortium selectively enriched by estrogen in females",
    "IsoUDCA promotes SREBP2 nuclear accumulation, upregulating STARD4 and CYP7B1",
    "This mechanism facilitates intracellular cholesterol handling, contributing to female hepatoprotection against CLD"
  ],
  "study_type": "Metabolomics database analysis",
  "scientific_significance": "The study identifies a microbiome-bile acid axis that explains sex-based differences in chronic liver disease susceptibility, providing a mechanistic basis for potential sex-specific therapeutic interventions."
}

{
  "summary": "This study addresses the challenge of achieving low threshold voltage (Vth) and high performance in n-type organic electrochemical transistors (OECTs). Using a stepwise cyanation strategy on quinoxaline-based polymers, the researchers developed QxCN-CNTVT, which exhibits a deep LUMO level (-4.23 eV), low Vth (0.13 V), high µC* (168.89 F cm-1 V-1 s-1), and stability. The material enables efficient amplification of electrooculogram (EOG) and electrocardiogram (ECG) signals and logic gate operations.",
  "key_findings": [
    "QxCN-CNTVT achieves a low threshold voltage of 0.13 V",
    "The polymer demonstrates a high µC* value of 168.89 F cm-1 V-1 s-1",
    "The material enables efficient amplification of EOG and ECG signals and logic gate functions"
  ],
  "study_type": "In vitro synthesis and device testing",
  "scientific_significance": "This work provides a molecular design strategy for n-type OECTs that simultaneously achieves low threshold voltage and high performance, advancing low-power bioelectronic applications."
}

{
  "summary": "This review examines the evolution and current state of occluders for interventional cardiovascular therapy, focusing on materials from non-degradable metals to biodegradable polymers. It highlights nitinol's role as a gold standard, surface nanocoatings for endothelialization, and design innovations in biodegradable devices. The study identifies key challenges in matching degradation rates with tissue healing and future directions for pediatric and elderly applications.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Nitinol's hyperelasticity enables self-expanding occluders as the current gold standard for structural heart disease.",
    "Surface nanocoatings of bioceramics like titanium nitride promote rapid endothelialization on nitinol implants.",
    "Biodegradable polymers such as polylactic acid and polydioxanone are designed with locking strategies to overcome hyperelasticity limitations."
  ],
  "study_type": "",
  "scientific_significance": "This review advances understanding of occluder material evolution and design principles, providing a foundation for safer, more effective interventional therapies in structural heart disease."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigates how Roman and early medieval migrations influenced Iberian demography using ancient DNA from 243 individuals spanning 100-800 CE. Genetic analysis reveals significant Eastern Mediterranean and North African ancestry influx during Roman rule, followed by Germanic and Alan group dominance post-5th century. The Hispano-Roman population maintained continuity despite regional demographic shifts, contrasting with Britain and the central Mediterranean where major transformations occurred.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Roman era: Eastern Mediterranean and North African ancestry influx in Iberia",
    "Post-5th century: Germanic and Alan groups assumed political control",
    "Hispano-Roman population maintained demographic continuity despite regional changes"
  ],
  "study_type": "Ancient DNA analysis",
  "scientific_significance": "The findings demonstrate regional variation in demographic continuity and admixture across post-Roman Europe, highlighting how Roman and early medieval migrations differentially shaped Iberian versus other European populations."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigates a strategy to activate inert Zn single-atom sites for oxygen reduction catalysis using rare-earth oxide clusters. By engineering long-range electronic modulation via LaOx clusters and porous carbon supports, the catalyst achieves a high half-wave potential of 0.90 V and stable alkaline performance. It demonstrates superior non-precious-metal electrocatalysis with a zinc-air battery power density of 177.3 mW cm-2.",
  "key_findings": [
    "LaOx clusters enable long-range electronic modulation of Zn sites to enhance oxygen reduction reaction activity",
    "The catalyst achieves a half-wave potential of 0.90 V and stable alkaline performance",
    "Zinc-air batteries using this catalyst reach 177.3 mW cm-2 power density at 225 mA cm-2"
  ],
  "study_type": "Electrocatalysis study",
  "scientific_significance": "This work establishes a rare-earth cluster-mediated approach for long-range electronic tuning of single-atom sites, advancing non-precious metal electrocatalysis for oxygen reduction reactions."
}

{
  "summary": "This study addresses early bacterial infection and poor osseointegration in orthopedic 316L stainless steel implants. It developed a nanostructured gradient Ta-O-F coating with a corrosion-stable Ta2O5 barrier and fluoride reservoir to regulate ion release. The coating improved corrosion resistance, moderated fluoride release, maintained antibacterial activity against pathogens, enhanced endothelial and osteoblastic responses in vitro, and reduced inflammation and bacterial load in infected rat implants compared to unmodified steel.",
  "key_findings": [
    "The nanostructured gradient Ta-O-F coating suppressed burst fluoride release and sustained antibacterial activity against S. aureus and E. coli",
    "The coating enhanced endothelial migration, tube formation, osteoblast adhesion, proliferation, ALP activity, and matrix mineralization in vitro",
    "In infected rat models, the coating reduced local inflammation and bacterial burden while improving peri-implant bone architecture"
  ],
  "study_type": "In vitro and in vivo studies",
  "scientific_significance": "This work demonstrates a barrier-reservoir strategy for synchronizing anti-infective and osteoregenerative responses on metallic implants, addressing a critical challenge in orthopedic implant failure."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigated proteomic changes in T cell exhaustion using multi-omic approaches including transcriptomics, quantitative proteomics, and ubiquitin proteomics. The research identified widespread proteostasis collapse, metabolic shifts, and DNA repair alterations, with E3 ubiquitin ligases correlating immunotherapy outcomes. Key findings include the role of proteostatic collapse in T cell dysfunction and the potential of E3 ligase manipulation to enhance T cell persistence.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Widespread proteostasis collapse in T cell exhaustion",
    "E3 ubiquitin ligases correlate with immunotherapy outcomes",
    "Overexpression of RNF1.66 enhances T cell persistence in vitro and in vivo"
  ],
  "study_type": "Multi-omic atlas construction",
  "scientific_significance": "The study establishes proteostatic collapse as a central mechanism in T cell exhaustion, providing a foundation for targeting E3 ligases to improve cancer immunotherapy efficacy."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigates re-functionalizing a titanium-based MOF with rhenium to create photochargeable materials for solar energy storage. The researchers designed ReCl47-bpy, which exhibits visible-light response, faster charge accumulation, and enhanced retention. Colloidal suspensions achieved 19.5 C g-1 storage capacity with 70% retention after two and a half months, enabling efficient dark hydrogen production. The material also functions as photoelectrodes for solar-driven charge storage.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Re-functionalized Ti-MOFs achieve 19.5 C g-1 electron storage capacity in colloidal suspensions under solar irradiation",
    "ReCl47-bpy retains 70% of stored charge after 2.5 months of storage",
    "The material enables electron-to-hydrogen conversion with efficiencies exceeding 90% in dark conditions"
  ],
  "study_type": "Photochargeable materials for solar energy storage",
  "scientific_significance": "This work demonstrates a strategy to enhance MOF-based solar energy storage through rhenium functionalization, enabling efficient charge retention and on-demand hydrogen production without direct light exposure."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigated enhancing NB-UVB phototherapy for psoriasis using microneedles with high UV transmittance (UVMNs) and calcipotriol delivery. The approach combined UVMNs to improve UV penetration and reduce dosage, with calcipot.riol coating to suppress keratinocyte proliferation and modulate immune responses. In psoriatic mice, this integrated therapy significantly reduced symptoms compared to conventional NB-UVB alone.",
  "key_findings": [
    "UVMNs increased NB-UVB penetration through psoriatic skin, reducing required irradiation dosage",
    "Calcipotriol release suppressed keratinocyte proliferation and modulated T-cell responses",
    "Integrated therapy improved psoriasis symptoms in mice without increasing photodamage risk"
  ],
  "study_type": "In vivo mouse model",
  "scientific_significance": "This work demonstrates a dual-modality approach that enhances phototherapy efficacy while minimizing risks, offering a potential pathway for more effective, patient-friendly psoriasis treatment."
}

{
  "summary": "This study identifies MCT6 as an intestinal transporter responsible for exporting N-lactoyl-phenylalanine (Lac-Phe), a metabolite linked to metformin-induced weight loss. Using genetic knockout and exogenous Lac-Phe supplementation, the research demonstrates MCT6's role in maintaining systemic Lac-Phe levels during metformin treatment. MCT6 deficiency impairs metformin-associated weight loss in high-fat diet mice, highlighting a transporter-mediated mechanism connecting gut metabolism to systemic metabolic effects.",
  "key_findings": [
    "MCT6 is an intestinal exporter of Lac-Phe, an anorexigenic metabolite",
    "MCT6 deletion blocks metformin-induced weight loss in high-fat diet mice",
    "Exogenous Lac-Phe rescues weight loss resistance in MCT6-knockout mice"
  ],
  "study_type": "Genetic knockout and in vitro transport assays",
  "scientific_significance": "This work establishes a direct link between intestinal transporter function and systemic metabolic regulation via a specific metabolite, revealing how local intracellular production translates to organism-wide metabolic effects."
}

{
  "summary": "This study addresses postoperative pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma management by engineering a nanobacterial platform that converts leaked pancreatic fluid into an antitumor resource. The system uses Bacteroides thetaiotaomicron to degrade tumor mucins and TRPV1 antagonists to suppress cancer-associated fibroblasts and enhance immune responses. It demonstrated reduced tumor recurrence, prolonged survival, and pain alleviation in models without systemic toxicity.",
  "key_findings": [
    "TNPs-N@B.t. + Gel enhanced tumor accumulation and suppressed local recurrence in postoperative PDAC models",
    "Bacteroides thetaiotaomicron degraded tumor-associated mucins to restore vulnerability to pancreatic enzymes",
    "TRPV1 antagonists attenuated cancer-associated fibroblast activation and promoted NK-cell antitumor responses"
  ],
  "study_type": "In vivo animal models",
  "scientific_significance": "This approach transforms pathological pancreatic fluid from a complication into a therapeutic resource, offering a novel strategy for postoperative PDAC management by leveraging the host microenvironment."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigates how spontaneous phase separation in Janus hydrogels spatiotemporally orchestrates fibrotic progression for functional tendon regeneration. The researchers engineered a Janus hydrogel (SPS-JH) using methacrylated hyaluronic acid and PLD polymers via spontaneous phase separation. The hydrogel's outer layer reduces adhesions while the inner layer provides mechanical reinforcement. SPS-JH releases COX-2 siRNA to inhibit fibroblast hyperactivation and niclosamide to polarize macrophages, thereby promoting functional tendon repair in rats with Achilles tendon injuries.",
  "key_findings": [
    "SPS-JH reduces peritendinous adhesions in rat Achilles tendon injuries",
    "SPS-JH releases COX-2 siRNA to silence fibroblast hyperactivation during early inflammation",
    "SPS-JH sustains niclosamide release to polarize macrophages and suppress fibrogenesis during proliferation"
  ],
  "study_type": "In vitro and in vivo rat model",
  "scientific_significance": "This work demonstrates a structurally minimalist hydrogel design that leverages spontaneous phase separation to dynamically coordinate fibrotic progression, enabling functional tendon regeneration without complex fabrication."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigates how metazoan lagging-strand DNA synthesis coordinates with leading-strand replication. Using single-molecule imaging in Xenopus extracts, researchers visualized polymerases Pol α and Pol δ at replication forks. They discovered neither polymerase remains stably attached to the replisome; instead, lagging-strand synthesis occurs distributively via rapid recruitment of new polymerase molecules per Okazaki fragment. This indicates leading- and lagging-strand synthesis operate independently without intrinsic coupling.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Lagging-strand synthesis in metazoans occurs distributively, requiring rapid recruitment of new Pol α and Pol δ for each Okazaki fragment",
    "Neither Pol α nor Pol δ is stably tethered to the replisome during replication",
    "Leading- and lagging-strand synthesis are not intrinsically coupled in metazoans"
  ],
  "study_type": "Single-molecule imaging in Xenopus nuclear extracts",
  "scientific_significance": "This work reveals that metazoans resolve the topological challenge of anti-parallel DNA replication by enabling distributive lagging-strand synthesis without active coordination between leading and lagging strands."
}

{
  "summary": "This study uses steady-state and transient absorption spectroscopy to distinguish spectral signatures and excited-state dynamics between noninteracting and interacting Y6 chromophores in solution and thin films. It identifies localized Frenkel excitons in noninteracting Y6 and aggregate excitons in interacting Y6, revealing fluence-dependent lifetimes for aggregates due to singlet-singlet annihilation and ultrafast polaron formation within tens of picoseconds.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Localized Frenkel-type excitons decay monoexponentially on nanosecond timescales in noninteracting Y6",
    "Aggregate excitons exhibit fluence-dependent lifetimes (30-200 ps) governed by singlet-singlet annihilation",
    "Polaron formation occurs within tens of picoseconds on aggregated Y6, persisting into the nanosecond regime"
  ],
  "study_type": "Transient absorption spectroscopy",
  "scientific_significance": "The findings establish a framework for interpreting transient absorption features in Y6 and reveal that polaron formation arises from Onsager-like autoionization of delocalized excitons in interacting domains, providing mechanistic insight into charge generation in nonfullerene acceptor solar cells."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigates a mechano-regenerative approach using YAP-primed engineered exosomes and active mechanical stimulation to enhance tendon-bone healing. The platform integrates ROS-responsive hydrogels with a self-contracting microneedle system for targeted delivery. Results demonstrated improved fibrocartilage formation, bone ingrowth, reduced bacterial burden in infected models, and restored biomechanical strength and function.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Engineered exosomes from YAP agonist-preconditioned cells promoted osteogenic and chondrogenic differentiation of stem cells",
    "ROS-responsive hydrogel accelerated fibrocartinage formation and bone ingrowth in tendon-bone healing",
    "Microneedle platform with mechanical stimulation reduced bacterial load and improved functional recovery in infected rotator cuff models"
  ],
  "study_type": "In vivo rabbit and rat models",
  "scientific_significance": "This work establishes a synergistic mechanism where exosome signaling and mechanical cues jointly accelerate tendon-bone regeneration, providing a template for mechano-regenerative therapies in complex tissue repair."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigates how lifestyle factors induce regulatory T cell fragility leading to immune-related adverse events (irAEs) during immune checkpoint blockade. Using a mouse model where diet alters gut microbiota and metabolism, researchers recreated human-like irAE susceptibility. Key findings include Akkermansia muciniphila enrichment driving Treg instability, sex and age-dependent severity, and dietary interventions reversing inflammation. The LISA model provides mechanistic insights into irAEs and potential strategies to mitigate toxicity.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Diet-induced gut dysbiosis with Akkermansia muciniphila enrichment causes regulatory T cell fragility",
    "Sex and age modulate the severity of immune-related adverse events",
    "Dietary interventions and interferon-γ blockade restore regulatory T cell stability and resolve inflammation"
  ],
  "study_type": "Mouse model",
  "scientific_significance": "This work establishes dietary and microbial factors as critical determinants of immune checkpoint blockade toxicity, offering a preclinical framework to enhance immunotherapy safety through targeted metabolic and immunological interventions."
}
